(e) PER-SEATING SCALE -- does a directory's loader `booting` median move with the same image's kernel segments?
  A common factor here can be the host's monotonic rate or the device's timebase; this data cannot separate them.
  H-prop (one rate factor per seating) predicts slope ~1 for every segment; refuted for a segment whose 95 % interval holds 0.
  H-add (USB latency, ms per read) predicts slope ~0 for a segment of >= 1 s; refuted for one whose interval excludes 0.
  ratio = the directory's median / the image's median of its per-directory medians; a segment's fit re-normalises both sides over the directories that hold it.
  the pairs are not independent (an image in two directories gives two points mirrored about 1): the interval is indicative.
  fit over every (image, directory) pair: ln(segment ratio) on ln(loader ratio), least squares with an intercept
    segment              from -> to                typical  pairs   slope      95 % interval       r  H-prop   H-add
  fit without id0=none, which pools several builds and is not one image: ln(segment ratio) on ln(loader ratio), least squares with an intercept
    segment              from -> to                typical  pairs   slope      95 % interval       r  H-prop   H-add
    no image occurs in two directories that also hold loader boots: nothing to fit

